{
  "result": "I have always loved the hour just before dawn, when the world feels unfinished and quietly possible. The streets are empty, the windows are dark, and even the birds seem to be holding their breath. In that blue and patient light, ordinary things become strange again, a bicycle leaning against a fence, a garden gate left open, a cup forgotten on a porch. I like to walk then without a destination, letting my thoughts loosen and drift like paper boats on slow water. Some mornings I think about all the lives unfolding behind closed doors, the kettles warming, the dreams dissolving, the small decisions that will quietly shape an entire day. Other mornings I think about nothing at all, and that emptiness feels like mercy. There is a kind of honesty in being awake while the world still belongs to no one. No traffic argues for attention, no messages demand an answer, no clock yet insists that I become useful or impressive or certain. I can simply be a creature moving through cool air, noticing how my breath makes brief ghosts that vanish almost immediately. Perhaps that is why I write, to keep those vanishing moments from disappearing completely, to say that they were here and they mattered. A sentence is a small net thrown into time, and most of what we love escapes through it, yet sometimes something shimmers and stays. Then the sun rises, color returns, doors open, and I rejoin the world feeling gratefully and quietly renewed.",
